{"id":"c0fc1c46-812f-4a59-abd6-879c11e87d2d","source_generation_id":"learn-out-c33ae7530662","source_type":"learn_document","title":"The lesson opens on the classroom plant on the windowsill beside the cup of water and asks learners to commit to a guess about where its food comes from, so a personal wrong model is on the table. The explanation then fixes the category term — the plant is a producer, a living thing that makes its own food — and separates what the plant takes in through its roots from what it makes. The contrast section puts the producer idea against the soil-as-food and water-as-food readings on the same plant so the boundary is visible, and the close checks whether the learner now recognizes the plant, not the soil or the water, as the maker of the food.","class_label":null,"created_at":"2026-09-13T20:59:36.551384","updated_at":"2026-09-13T21:03:41.789921","document":{"id":"c0fc1c46-812f-4a59-abd6-879c11e87d2d","nodes":[{"id":"learn-node:orient-b1:paragraph:0","kind":"paragraph","text":"Look at the potted plant on the classroom windowsill. Beside it sits a cup of water. Before we explain anything, think about this: where do you think the plant's food comes from? Settle on one answer of your own and be ready to commit to it.","teaching_block_id":"orient-b1"},{"id":"learn-node:orient-b1:callout:1","body":"Look at the potted plant on the windowsill. Beside it sits a cup of water. Before we explain anything, decide for yourself: where do you think this plant's food comes from? Commit to one answer now — you will check it against the lesson's explanation later.","kind":"callout","tone":"note","title":"Where does the plant's food come from?","teaching_block_id":"orient-b1"},{"id":"learn-node:explain-b1:heading:2","kind":"heading","text":"Producers: Living Things That Make Their Own Food","level":2,"teaching_block_id":"explain-b1"},{"id":"learn-node:explain-b1:paragraph:3","kind":"paragraph","text":"A producer is a living thing that makes its own food. The plant on the windowsill is a producer: its food is made by the plant itself, not delivered to it from outside. [T12-CORR-MARKER]","teaching_block_id":"explain-b1"},{"id":"learn-node:explain-b2:paragraph:4","kind":"paragraph","text":"Look at the plant on the windowsill: roots, stems and leaves, alive and growing. Its roots draw soil materials and water into the plant, but what enters through the roots is raw material the plant uses — not the plant's food. The food itself is made by the plant, inside the plant. That is what it means to call the plant a producer.","teaching_block_id":"explain-b2"},{"id":"learn-node:explain-b2:list:5","kind":"list","items":["What the windowsill plant takes in: its roots draw in water and materials from the soil. These are raw materials the plant uses — they are not the plant's food.","What the windowsill plant makes: the food itself is made inside the plant. The plant is the maker of its own food, which is why it is called a producer."],"ordered":false,"teaching_block_id":"explain-b2"},{"id":"learn-node:contrast-b1:paragraph:6","kind":"paragraph","text":"Look again at the plant on the windowsill. One learner claims its food is the soil, pulled up through the roots, so the soil is what feeds it. Set that next to the producer idea and the soil reading falls apart: the plant is a producer, a living thing that makes its own food inside itself, and the soil is only one of the things it takes in — a raw material, not the food. If the soil were the food, the plant would not need to make anything at all, yet it is the plant itself, not the soil, that does the making.","teaching_block_id":"contrast-b1"},{"id":"learn-node:contrast-b1:callout:7","body":"Look at the windowsill plant. It is true that the roots draw material up from the soil, but that material is not the plant's food. The plant is a producer: it makes its own food inside itself. The soil only supplies some of the raw materials the plant takes in. So the reading that the plant's food is soil pulled up through the roots cannot stand — the plant still makes the food, and the soil is only one of its intakes.","kind":"callout","tone":"warning","title":"The Soil Is Not the Plant's Food","teaching_block_id":"contrast-b1"},{"id":"learn-node:contrast-b1:paragraph:8","kind":"paragraph","text":"A learner might say, looking at our windowsill plant, that its food is the soil, pulled up through the roots. If that were so, the plant's food would come from outside it and the plant would simply be a consumer of soil. But the plant makes its own food inside itself — that is what being a producer means. Soil is one of the plant's intakes through its roots, not the food itself, so the soil-as-food reading cannot stand.","teaching_block_id":"contrast-b1"},{"id":"learn-node:contrast-b2:heading:9","kind":"heading","text":"Is the Water We Pour the Plant's Food?","level":3,"teaching_block_id":"contrast-b2"},{"id":"learn-node:contrast-b2:paragraph:10","kind":"paragraph","text":"Look again at the windowsill plant and the cup of water beside it. One learner says the plant's food is the water we pour in — after all, water is what we add. But the plant on that windowsill is a producer: a living thing that makes its own food. Water is something the plant takes in, not the food itself, so pouring in more water does not feed the plant in the food sense. The water goes in, and the plant remains the maker of its own food.","teaching_block_id":"contrast-b2"},{"id":"learn-node:contrast-b2:callout:11","body":"It is tempting to say the windowsill plant's food is the water beside it, since water is what you pour in. But water is only something the plant takes in through its roots — it is not the food. The plant makes its own food, so it remains the maker. Pouring in more water does not feed the plant in the food sense; it only adds more of what the plant takes in.","kind":"callout","tone":"note","title":"Water is not the plant's food","teaching_block_id":"contrast-b2"},{"id":"learn-node:check-b1:paragraph:12","kind":"paragraph","text":"Look again at the classroom plant on the windowsill. Its roots sit in soil, and water has been poured in around them. Before you go, decide: where does this plant's food come from? Write one sentence that names the source of the plant's food, and then add one more sentence that states whether the plant is the thing that makes that food.","teaching_block_id":"check-b1"},{"id":"learn-node:orient-b1:choice:13","kind":"interaction","config":{"options":[{"id":"soil","text":"From the soil in the pot","explanation":"This treats soil as the plant's food rather than as the material the roots anchor the plant in."},{"id":"water","text":"From the water beside it","explanation":"This treats water as food, but water is one of the things a plant takes in rather than the food it makes."},{"id":"sunlight","text":"From the sunlight through the window","explanation":"This treats light as food, but light supplies the energy for making food, not the food itself."},{"id":"air","text":"From the air around it","explanation":"This treats a gas in the air as food, but a plant takes in air and still must make its own food."}],"presentation":"text","correct_option_id":"soil"},"prompt":"A potted plant sits on the windowsill next to a cup of water. Where do you think the plant's food comes from?","contract":{"id":"learn-node:orient-b1:choice:13","kind":"choice","config":{"options":[{"id":"soil","text":"From the soil in the pot","explanation":"This treats soil as the plant's food rather than as the material the roots anchor the plant in."},{"id":"water","text":"From the water beside it","explanation":"This treats water as food, but water is one of the things a plant takes in rather than the food it makes."},{"id":"sunlight","text":"From the sunlight through the window","explanation":"This treats light as food, but light supplies the energy for making food, not the food itself."},{"id":"air","text":"From the air around it","explanation":"This treats a gas in the air as food, but a plant takes in air and still must make its own food."}],"presentation":"text","correct_option_id":"soil"},"prompt":"A potted plant sits on the windowsill next to a cup of water. Where do you think the plant's food comes from?","feedback":{"correct":"Good — you committed to an idea about where the plant's food comes from, and we will put it to the test as the lesson goes on.","incorrect":"You committed to one idea about where the plant's food comes from. Hold onto it and check it against the explanation that follows."},"completion":{"type":"submitted"},"provenance":{"policy":{"input_revision":null,"policy_version":"1.0.0","definition_hash":"9c9eca46dacb6c6603192a541ee7c019236a8ab713137ae9027d3e3557c1c7eb","fallback_reason":null,"definition_version":"2.0.0","input_availability":"supplied_statements","executed_knowledge_mode":"supplied_facts","knowledge_policy_source":"package:1.0.0","assessment_policy_source":"package:1.0.0","executed_evaluation_mode":"accepted-answers","teacher_review_permitted":false,"effective_knowledge_policy":"supplied_preferred","requested_knowledge_policy":null,"effective_assessment_policy":"automatic_required","requested_assessment_policy":null},"block_id":"orient-b1","input_hash":"c11abf993eb95d4fdd58437d9f4a75c298474770b85b0bd02a3a905c1a43304e","payload_hash":"12931d8123cb8c4129ca3ee12977325d016ea894d1045ab6e512a5addb34d175","capability_id":"choice","work_order_id":"learn::request::choice","authoring_mode":"generate","definition_hash":"9c9eca46dacb6c6603192a541ee7c019236a8ab713137ae9027d3e3557c1c7eb","teaching_plan_id":"c538c330-e276-4aeb-8ff8-278464043dd9","source_identities":[],"teaching_revision":1,"teaching_plan_hash":"ad-hoc","teaching_plan_revision":1,"capability_contract_hash":"9c9eca46dacb6c6603192a541ee7c019236a8ab713137ae9027d3e3557c1c7eb"},"concept_refs":[],"accessibility":{"narration":"optional","aria_label":"A potted plant sits on the windowsill next to a cup of water. Where do you think th